Apple could offer a limited-edition iPhone 20 Pro model that incorporates ceramic, according to the Chinese leaker known as Fixed Focus Digital.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/01/Mostly-Screen-iPhone-Purple-Feature-2027.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1033225" /><br/>
In a <a href="https://weibo.com/5821279480/Rt9k9lDUT">post on Weibo</a>, the leaker claimed to have gotten their hands on ceramic components for the 20th-anniversary iPhone, which is expected to launch next year. The leaker said that production capacity for the components is planned to be very low, suggesting they will be for a limited-edition model.<br/>
<br/>
In a follow-up comment, the leaker claimed that the project would enter mass production if it passes engineering validation testing (EVT), and even joked that its scarcity could make it appreciate in value.<br/>
<br/>
The leaker doesn't say what kind of ceramic components they have in their possession, leaving it unclear whether Apple is considering a ceramic rear panel, a larger section of the enclosure, or a smaller exterior part.<br/>
<br/>
Apple already offers ceramic as a premium casing material on the Apple Watch Series 12, which comes in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/apple-watch-series-12-comes-new-finishes/">Pearl White and Night Blue ceramic</a> alongside aluminum and titanium options. The company also previously sold ceramic Apple Watch Edition models, including a white ceramic version of the Series 5.<br/>
<br/>
Interestingly, the ‌iPhone Air‌ features Ceramic Shield 2 on the front and Ceramic Shield on the back, and Fixed Focus Digital has previously said next year's iPhone 20 Pro models will <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/16/iphone-20-pro-to-be-based-on-iphone-air/">use the same glass design</a>, but with more pronounced curves along all four sides of a titanium frame. Ceramic Shield incorporates tiny ceramic crystals within a glass matrix, which preserves transparency while improving durability. However, the wording of the leaker's latest post suggests that ceramic could be applied in a more visually prominent way. <br/>
<br/>
